---
import NewsletterSignup from "../components/NewsletterSignup.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const description = "How SkillCheck reads skills, what it stores, and how the labels work.";
---

<Base title="About SkillCheck" description={description}>
  <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    SkillCheck is a static index. It records metadata, a compatibility reading, GitHub activity, and scan flags.
    It links to the source. It does not host or mirror skill code.
  </p>

  <section class="card panel">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>
      A nightly job reads the public ClawHub catalog and the public skills.sh sitemap. For each skill it fetches
      SKILL.md, and script files when a GitHub tree lists them, then discards that text after analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compatibility rules live in versioned YAML. Each agent page cites the spec it was checked against.
      The labels are compatible, likely, and needs changes. The reason is shown next to the label.
    </p>
    <p>
      GitHub fields are stars, last push, open issues and pull requests, license, and archived state.
      Health is active when the repo is not archived and the last push was within 365 days. Quiet means the last
      push is older. Unknown means there is no repo data. The job caches GitHub responses and skips a repo when
      the ETag has not changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scan looks for a pipe into a shell, a base64 blob, a credential or keychain path, and an obfuscated URL.
      The page says "scanned, no flags" or lists the flags. It never says a skill is safe.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel" style="margin-top: 1rem">
    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics are optional. Nothing analytics-related loads until you choose Allow analytics. If enabled,
      SkillCheck uses aggregate visit measurement without Google advertising signals or personalization. GA4 records
      query-free page location and referrer values. You can reopen the choice at any time and turn analytics off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel" style="margin-top: 1rem">
    <h2>Quality score</h2>
    <p>
      The home table sorts by a score from 0 to 100. It is a reading of public numbers, not a judgment that a skill is good to run.
    </p>
    <p>
      Popularity is 70 points. ClawHub skills use download counts. skills.sh skills use GitHub stars. Each source is scaled on its own with a log, so one very popular listing does not flatten the rest of that source. A skill at the top of its source gets the full 70. A skill with none of that signal gets 0.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recency is 20 points when the date we have is within 90 days, and 12 points when it is within 18 months (540 days). ClawHub uses the catalog update time. skills.sh uses the GitHub last push. No date means no recency points.
    </p>
    <p>
      A description of at least 40 characters adds 10 points. Each scan flag subtracts 15, up to 40. The score does not go below 0.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ingest keeps ClawHub skills with at least 1,000 downloads and a real description plus a body of at least 80 characters. It keeps up to 200 skills.sh skills from repos with at least 10 GitHub stars, not archived, and pushed within 18 months. Flagged skills stay in the catalog. The home list hides them until you set Scan to "Flags found" or "Any". Search still opens their pages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card panel" style="margin-top: 1rem">
    <h2>What this is for</h2>
    <p>
      The reading is neutral across Hermes, OpenClaw, Claude Code, and Codex. You can see why a skill landed on
      a label instead of trusting a single-agent gallery.
    </p>
    <p>
      cultofclaude.com and skillagent.dev are other places people browse skills. SkillCheck is the cross-agent
      reading with the reasons left visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <NewsletterSignup />
</Base>
